Add this input state and pause flag, replacing the original `onkeydown` line and wrapping the animation body with the guard:

```js
let paused=false;onkeydown=e=>{if(e.key==='p')paused=!paused;if(e.key==='ArrowUp'||e.key==='w')p-=20;if(e.key==='ArrowDown'||e.key==='s')p+=20};
function f(){if(paused){x.fillStyle='white';x.fillText('PAUSED',285,180);requestAnimationFrame(f);return;} /* keep the rest of the original frame body here */}
```
For a drop-in version, put `if(paused)return` at the top of your existing frame function and call it again when toggling pause.
